# Prompts

What a model is asked, for each kind of unit.

Every prompt is built from the same parts, so a model always finds things in the
same place: a SYSTEM message that says what it is doing and carries the whole
kernel reference (`reference.lg`), and a USER message with the problem, the
accepted statement document (when there is one), the trail digest (what was
already tried and why it failed), the verified lemmas it may cite, the strategy's
playbook, the task itself, and the output contract.

The prompt is data the strategy shapes — the playbook and the sampling budget are
the strategy's — but the parts that keep the factory honest are not the
strategy's to change: the reference, the output contract and the acceptance
rules are written here and read by `submission.lg` and `verdict.lg`, the same
words on both sides.

Import Shared.
Import Submission.
Import OpenRouter from "openrouter.lg".
Import Reference.

## Note
Everything a prompt may draw on. `statement` is the accepted statement document
(`""` before there is one), `previous` and `diagnostics` the last submission and
what the kernel said about it (for a repair), and `failedBlock` the text of the
block the kernel refused.

## A PromptContext has:
    a problem, which is Problem.
    a statement, which is Text.
    a goalName, which is Text.
    a trailDigest, which is Text.
    a lemmas, which is Seq of Lemma.
    a playbook, which is Text.
    a previous, which is Text.
    a diagnostics, which is Text.
    a failedBlock, which is Text.

## Note
A prompt: the unit kind it is for, and its two messages.

## A Prompt has:
    a kind, which is Text.
    a system, which is Text.
    a user, which is Text.

## Note
The opening of every system message.

## To roleText (kind: Text) -> Text:
    If kind is equal to "review":
        Return "You review formal statements of mathematical problems. You are independent of whoever wrote the statement, and you are paid for catching a statement that does not say what the problem says, not for agreeing.".
    If kind is equal to "summarize_trail":
        Return "You keep the research log of a mathematical problem. You turn a long record of attempts into what the next person needs: what is known, what failed and why, and what to try next.".
    Return "You are a proof engineer. You write proofs and formal statements in the LOGOS kernel vernacular, and every one is checked by the LOGOS kernel: nothing counts until it checks.".

## Note
The system message: the role, then (for every unit that writes vernacular) the
reference. The reviewer gets the reference too, so it can read the statement.

## To systemText (kind: Text) -> Text:
    Return roleText(kind) + "\n\n" + kernelReference().

## Note
The problem as the model reads it: identity, title, source, the informal
statement, and the Lean statement when there is one.

## To problemSection (p: Problem) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "## The problem\n\n" + p's id + " — " + p's title + "\nSource: " + p's source + ", collection " + p's collection + ", category " + p's category + ".".
    If p's statementInformal is not equal to "":
        Set out to out + "\n\n" + p's statementInformal.
    Inspect p's leanStatement:
        When Some (lean):
            Set out to out + "\n\nThe Lean statement:\n\n```lean\n" + lean + "\n```".
        When None:
            Set out to out + "\n\nThere is no formal statement yet.".
    Return out.

## Note
The accepted statement, verbatim, and what a proof must be.

## To statementSection (c: PromptContext) -> Text:
    If c's statement is equal to "":
        Return "".
    Return "## The statement\n\nThis document is checked first, exactly as written. It defines `" + c's goalName + "`, the proposition to prove.\n\n```logos-kernel\n" + c's statement + "\n```".

## Note
The lemmas already verified for this problem, each a statement the model may
restate and cite as an `Axiom`-free theorem of its own.

## To lemmaSection (lemmas: Seq of Lemma) -> Text:
    If length of lemmas is equal to 0:
        Return "".
    Let mutable lines be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for l in lemmas:
        Push "- `" + l's name + "` (" + l's status + "): " + l's statement to lines.
    Return "## Verified lemmas\n\nThese are proved. To use one, include its statement and proof in your block under a new name.\n\n" + join(lines, "\n").

## Note
The trail digest, under its heading.

## To trailSection (c: PromptContext) -> Text:
    Return "## What has been tried\n\n" + c's trailDigest.

## Note
The strategy's playbook, under its heading, when it has one.

## To playbookSection (c: PromptContext) -> Text:
    If c's playbook is equal to "":
        Return "".
    Return "## Playbook\n\n" + c's playbook.

## Note
What the unit asks for, in words.

## To taskText (kind: Text, c: PromptContext) -> Text:
    Let proofName be proofNameFor(c's goalName).
    If kind is equal to "formalize":
        Return "## Task\n\nWrite the statement of this problem as a kernel-vernacular document. Define every notion it needs with `Definition` or `Inductive`, and end with `Definition " + c's goalName + " : Prop := …` saying exactly what the problem says — no more, no less. Do not prove anything and declare no `Axiom`: a statement asserts nothing. Prefer the prelude's own types (`Nat`, `Int`, `List`) to new ones.".
    If kind is equal to "prove":
        Return "## Task\n\nProve `" + c's goalName + "`. Your block may define helper lemmas first; it must contain `Theorem " + proofName + " : " + c's goalName + " := …`.".
    If kind is equal to "decompose":
        Return "## Task\n\nDo not prove `" + c's goalName + "` outright. Split it into lemmas, each stated as an `Axiom` with a short comment saying why it should hold, and prove `Theorem " + proofName + " : " + c's goalName + " := …` from them. Each lemma becomes a problem of its own, so make each one smaller and more concrete than the goal.".
    If kind is equal to "special_case":
        Return "## Task\n\nState a special case of `" + c's goalName + "` that a computation can settle — the claim for every n up to a bound, a fixed small instance — as `Definition special_goal : Prop := …`, and prove it as `Theorem proof_of_special_goal : special_goal := …`, by `decide` or by computation where you can.".
    If kind is equal to "counterexample":
        Return "## Task\n\nLook for a counterexample. If you find one, prove `Not " + c's goalName + "` as `Theorem refutation_of_" + c's goalName + " : Not " + c's goalName + " := …`; if you find none, say so in `Summary:` and give what you checked in `Next:` lines.".
    If kind is equal to "repair":
        Return "## Task\n\nThe submission below was refused by the kernel. Fix it so that it checks, keeping what was right. It must still contain `Theorem " + proofName + " : " + c's goalName + " := …`.\n\n```logos-kernel\n" + c's previous + "\n```\n\nThe kernel refused the block\n\n```logos-kernel\n" + c's failedBlock + "\n```\n\nwith\n\n```text\n" + c's diagnostics + "\n```".
    If kind is equal to "review":
        Return "## Task\n\nRead the statement document above as mathematics. First, paraphrase `" + c's goalName + "` in plain mathematical English from the document alone. Then compare your paraphrase with the problem as stated and decide whether the document says the same thing: not a special case, not a stronger claim, not a vacuous or trivially true one, with every quantifier and every definition meaning what the problem means.".
    If kind is equal to "summarize_trail":
        Return "## Task\n\nSummarize the attempts above for the next attempt: what is established, which approaches failed and why, and the two or three most promising next steps.".
    Return "## Task\n\nWork on `" + c's goalName + "`.".

## Note
How the model must answer, for each unit kind. Every vernacular-writing unit
answers with one `logos-kernel` block; the reviewer answers with one `review`
block, which `extractReview` reads; the summarizer answers in plain lines.

## To contractText (kind: Text) -> Text:
    If kind is equal to "review":
        Return "## Answer\n\nAnswer with exactly one fenced block opened by ```review, holding three lines:\n\n```review\nverdict: faithful | unfaithful | unsure\nparaphrase: <your plain-English reading of the goal>\nissues: <what differs from the problem, or none>\n```".
    If kind is equal to "summarize_trail":
        Return "## Answer\n\nAnswer with one line `Summary: …` and up to three lines `Next: …`, and nothing else.".
    Return "## Answer\n\nAnswer with exactly one fenced block opened by ```logos-kernel and closed by ```. After it, write one line `Summary: …` saying what your attempt does, and `Next: …` lines for what to try if it fails. The rules of a submission in the reference apply.".

## Note
The user message: every section that has something in it, in a fixed order.

## To userText (kind: Text, c: PromptContext) -> Text:
    Let mutable parts be a new Seq of Text.
    Push problemSection(c's problem) to parts.
    Repeat for section in [statementSection(c), trailSection(c), lemmaSection(c's lemmas), playbookSection(c)]:
        If section is not equal to "":
            Push section to parts.
    Push taskText(kind, c) to parts.
    Push contractText(kind) to parts.
    Return join(parts, "\n\n").

## Note
The prompt for a unit of `kind` in context `c`.

## To buildPrompt (kind: Text, c: PromptContext) -> Prompt:
    Return a new Prompt with kind kind and system systemText(kind) and user userText(kind, c).

## Note
A prompt as the request a strategy sends.

## To promptRequest (p: Prompt, s: Strategy) -> ChatRequest:
    Return a new ChatRequest with model s's model and messages [a new ChatMessage with role "system" and content p's system, a new ChatMessage with role "user" and content p's user] and maxTokens s's maxTokens and temperatureBp s's temperatureBp.

## Note
What a reviewer answered: its verdict (`faithful`, `unfaithful` or `unsure`), its
paraphrase and the issues it saw, or why the answer is not a review.

## A Review has:
    a verdict, which is Text.
    a paraphrase, which is Text.
    an issues, which is Text.
    a problem, which is Text.

## Note
The review in a reviewer's answer: the one ```review block, its three lines. A
verdict outside the three words is not a review, so an unclear reviewer can
never be counted as agreeing.

## To extractReview (answer: Text) -> Review:
    Let mutable inside be false.
    Let mutable found be 0.
    Let mutable verdict be "".
    Let mutable paraphrase be "".
    Let mutable issues be "".
    Repeat for line in lines(answer):
        If inside:
            If closesFence(line):
                Set inside to false.
            Otherwise:
                Let t be trim(line).
                If startsWith(t, "verdict:"):
                    Set verdict to trim(removePrefix(t, "verdict:")).
                If startsWith(t, "paraphrase:"):
                    Set paraphrase to trim(removePrefix(t, "paraphrase:")).
                If startsWith(t, "issues:"):
                    Set issues to trim(removePrefix(t, "issues:")).
        Otherwise:
            If opensFence(line, "review"):
                Set inside to true.
                Set found to found + 1.
    Let mutable problem be "".
    If ["faithful", "unfaithful", "unsure"] contains verdict is equal to false:
        Set problem to "the review's verdict is `{verdict}`, not faithful, unfaithful or unsure".
    If found is not equal to 1:
        Set problem to "the answer has {found} ```review blocks; exactly one is required".
    Return a new Review with verdict verdict and paraphrase paraphrase and issues issues and problem problem.

## Note
A context for the tests: the fixture statement, an empty trail, no lemmas.

## To sampleContext () -> PromptContext:
    Return a new PromptContext with problem sampleProblem() and statement fixtureStatement() and goalName "goal" and trailDigest "Trail of op:OP022: 0 entries. No attempt has been made yet." and lemmas [] and playbook "Try induction on the first natural number." and previous "" and diagnostics "" and failedBlock "".

## Test: a prove prompt carries the reference, the statement, the trail and the contract

Let p be buildPrompt("prove", sampleContext()).
Expect that p's system contains "# The LOGOS kernel vernacular".
Expect that p's system contains "## The rules of a submission".
Expect that p's user contains "## The problem".
Expect that p's user contains fixtureStatement().
Expect that p's user contains "## What has been tried".
Expect that p's user contains "## Playbook\n\nTry induction on the first natural number.".
Expect that p's user contains "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := …".
Expect that p's user contains "```logos-kernel and closed by ```".

## Test: the sections come in a fixed order

Let u be (buildPrompt("prove", sampleContext()))'s user.
Expect that indexOf(u, "## The problem") is less than indexOf(u, "## The statement").
Expect that indexOf(u, "## The statement") is less than indexOf(u, "## What has been tried").
Expect that indexOf(u, "## What has been tried") is less than indexOf(u, "## Task").
Expect that indexOf(u, "## Task") is less than indexOf(u, "## Answer").

## Test: a formalize prompt has no statement yet and asks for one

Let mutable c be sampleContext().
Set c's statement to "".
Let u be (buildPrompt("formalize", c))'s user.
Expect that u contains "## The statement" is equal to false.
Expect that u contains "Definition goal : Prop := …".
Expect that u contains "The Lean statement:".

## Test: an informal problem says it has no formal statement

Let mutable c be sampleContext().
Let mutable p be c's problem.
Set p's leanStatement to none.
Set c's problem to p.
Expect that (buildPrompt("formalize", c))'s user contains "There is no formal statement yet.".

## Test: a repair prompt shows the refused submission, the refused block and the error

Let mutable c be sampleContext().
Set c's previous to "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Set c's failedBlock to "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Set c's diagnostics to "Tactic error: step 2 (`rfl`): `rfl` does not apply: Nat_add Zero n and n do not convert".
Let u be (buildPrompt("repair", c))'s user.
Expect that u contains "The kernel refused the block".
Expect that u contains "do not convert".
Expect that u contains "```logos-kernel\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.\n```".

## Test: verified lemmas are listed when there are some

Let mutable c be sampleContext().
Set c's lemmas to [a new Lemma with id "lemma:1" and name "succ_step" and statement "forall (k : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero (Succ k)) (Succ (Nat_add Zero k))" and statementSha "s" and problemId "op:OP022" and documentSha "d" and status "verified" and axioms []].
Let u be (buildPrompt("prove", c))'s user.
Expect that u contains "## Verified lemmas".
Expect that u contains "- `succ_step` (verified): forall (k : Nat)".
Expect that (buildPrompt("prove", sampleContext()))'s user contains "## Verified lemmas" is equal to false.

## Test: every unit kind that writes vernacular names the theorem or definition it wants

Expect that (buildPrompt("decompose", sampleContext()))'s user contains "stated as an `Axiom`".
Expect that (buildPrompt("special_case", sampleContext()))'s user contains "Theorem proof_of_special_goal : special_goal".
Expect that (buildPrompt("counterexample", sampleContext()))'s user contains "Theorem refutation_of_goal : Not goal".

## Test: the reviewer and the summarizer get their own roles and contracts

Let r be buildPrompt("review", sampleContext()).
Expect that r's system contains "You review formal statements".
Expect that r's user contains "```review".
Expect that r's user contains "paraphrase `goal` in plain mathematical English from the document alone".
Let s be buildPrompt("summarize_trail", sampleContext()).
Expect that s's user contains "Summary: …".
Expect that s's system contains "research log".

## Test: a prompt becomes the strategy's request

Let st be a new Strategy with id "s1" and version 1 and parentId none and unitKind "prove" and model "openai/gpt-4o-mini" and temperatureBp 2000 and maxTokens 4096 and playbook "" and contextRules "" and status "incumbent" and createdAt 0.
Let p be buildPrompt("prove", sampleContext()).
Let r be promptRequest(p, st).
Expect that r's model is equal to "openai/gpt-4o-mini".
Expect that length of r's messages is equal to 2.
Expect that (item 1 of r's messages)'s role is equal to "system".
Expect that (item 2 of r's messages)'s content is equal to p's user.
Expect that r's maxTokens is equal to 4096.

## Test: a model's answer to a prove prompt is read by the submission extractor

Let answer be "```logos-kernel\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; induction n as [ | k ih]; rfl; rw [ih]; rfl.\n```\nSummary: induction on n.\nNext: prove the successor step separately.".
Let x be extractSubmission(answer).
Expect that x's problem is equal to "".
Expect that submissionProblems(x's block, "proof_of_goal", "goal") is equal to [].
Expect that x's suggestions is equal to ["prove the successor step separately."].

## Test: a review is read from its block, and a vague one is refused

Let good be extractReview("I read it.\n```review\nverdict: faithful\nparaphrase: every natural n satisfies 0 + n = n\nissues: none\n```").
Expect that good's problem is equal to "".
Expect that good's verdict is equal to "faithful".
Expect that good's paraphrase is equal to "every natural n satisfies 0 + n = n".
Let vague be extractReview("```review\nverdict: probably fine\n```").
Expect that vague's problem is equal to "the review's verdict is `probably fine`, not faithful, unfaithful or unsure".
Let missing be extractReview("verdict: faithful").
Expect that missing's problem is equal to "the answer has 0 ```review blocks; exactly one is required".
